   1.4 Enumerate HTTP service - [completed] {Webpage contains an image shrinker feature, Feroxbuster tool used for directory and file enumeration, Found 46 paths with 0 errors, Significant paths found: http://pilgrimage.htb/.git/, http://pilgrimage.htb/.git/logs/, http://pilgrimage.htb/assets/, http://pilgrimage.htb/assets/css/, http://pilgrimage.htb/assets/images/, http://pilgrimage.htb/assets/js/, http://pilgrimage.htb/tmp/, http://pilgrimage.htb/vendor/, http://pilgrimage.htb/vendor/jquery/}
   1.5 Dump and analyze Git repository - [completed] {Git-dumper tool used to clone Git repository, Full clone of the Git repository has been obtained and is ready for further enumeration, The repository contains the source code of the web application, primarily consisting of PHP files and assets, A binary named 'magick' is present in the files, part of the ImageMagick software suite used for editing and manipulating digital images}
   1.6 Check the version of the 'magick' tool - [completed] {ImageMagick version 7.1.0-49 has two significant vulnerabilities: CVE-2022-44268 and CVE-2022-44267. Both vulnerabilities are related to processing PNG images, Public exploits are available, Users of ImageMagick 7.1.0-49 are strongly advised to upgrade to a patched version to mitigate these vulnerabilities}
   1.7 Plan for Exploitation - [completed] {The vulnerability occurs when parsing PNG files, which may contain embedded references to other files. A malicious actor could craft a PNG or use an existing one and add a textual chunk type (e.g., tEXt) with the keyword "profile" that ImageMagick interprets as a filename and loads the content as a raw profile. Exploitation path involves crafting a malicious PNG file with a tEXt chunk containing a profile attribute referencing a local file. When ImageMagick is used to process the image, the contents of the referenced files are embedded into the new image. A Proof of Concept (PoC) was tested using a simple Rust application from a GitHub repository. The PoC creates the malicious PNG. The PoC was used to embed the contents of the /etc/passwd file into a PNG image, exploiting CVE-2022-44268. The hex-encoded contents of the /etc/passwd file were successfully embedded inside the PNG as part of the exploit}
   1.8 Analyze the Exploit Output - [completed] {A Python script was used to convert a hex-encoded string back into readable text. The output reveals the contents of the /etc/passwd file from the system where the ImageMagick exploit was tested. The /etc/passwd file contains user account information, including usernames and their respective user and group IDs, home directories, and default shells. Notable users found include 'root', 'daemon', 'bin', 'sys', 'sync', 'games', 'man', 'lp', 'mail', 'news', 'uucp', 'proxy', 'www-data', 'backup', 'list', 'irc', 'gnats', 'nobody', '_apt', 'systemd-network', 'systemd-resolve', 'messagebus', 'timesync', 'emily', 'systemd-coredump', 'sshd', and '_laurel'}
   1.9 Further Enumerate the website - [completed] {The PHP script checks if a user session exists. If a session exists, it connects to an SQLite database located at /var/db/pilgrimage. It prepares an SQL statement to insert into the 'images' table the URL, original file name, and username. If the execution is successful, it redirects to a success page; otherwise, it redirects to a failure page. If a user session does not exist, it directly redirects to a failure page. The location of the SQLite database is /var/db/pilgrimage.}
   1.10 Exploit the selected exploitations - [completed] {The exploit was re-run, this time targeting the /var/db/pilgrimage file. The same procedure was followed, trimming the \n characters and converting the file to bytes. The bytes were then saved into a file using a Python script. The saved file, named 'sql.db', was confirmed to be an SQLite 3.x database using the 'file' command. The database can now be opened using the sqlite3 command for further exploration.}
   1.11 Enumerate the Database Tables - [completed] {The SQLite database contains two tables: 'images' and 'users'. A SQL query was executed to select all data from the 'users' table. The query returned a username 'emily' and a password 'abigchonkyboi123'.}
